Rationing the Interconnection Queue: 
Governing Uncertainty under Reliability Urgency 
by 
Jiao Zhao 
Submitted to the Department of Urban Studies and Planning on May 15, 2026 in 
Partial Fulfillment of the Requirements for the Degree of Master in City Planning 
 
ABSTRACT 
Interconnection queues, managed by regional grid operators, determine how 
generators connect to the electric transmission grid. Over the past decade, these 
queues have become increasingly backlogged. As projected load growth and resource 
retirements intensified reliability concerns, grid operators began proposing queue-
rationing mechanisms to prioritize certain projects outside the standard 
interconnection process. This thesis examines two such mechanisms, approved by the 
Federal Energy Regulatory Commission in 2025 after contested proceedings: PJM’s 
one-time, score-based Reliability Resource Initiative and MISO’s three-year Expedited 
Resource Adequacy Study. It asks: when interconnection policymaking responds to 
reliability urgency, what uncertainties emerge, and how are they understood and 
managed? 
Drawing on docket documents, interviews, and queue data, this thesis shows that 
reliability urgency narrowed how uncertainty was managed. Docket analysis finds that 
formal regulatory contestation centered on uncertainties about reliability forecasts, 
cost shifting, and the ability of selection criteria to identify projects likely to deliver 
reliability benefits. Although these uncertainties reflected long-standing problems in 
interconnection policymaking, they were addressed under compressed timelines 
through substantial deference to RTO expertise. Interviews show that the formal 
debate left other uncertainties outside the regulatory frame, including the scope of 
reliability needs, the precedent set by repeated procedural departures, and the 
environmental consequences of reliability-driven project selection. Queue 
composition analysis further shows that rationing translated reliability urgency into 
queues distinct from the standard process, with disproportionate shares of gas 
resources and substantial concentrations of data-center-driven and utility-led 
projects. 
Taken together, these findings suggest that the short-term reliability concept used to 
justify queue rationing is too narrow to govern interconnection amid energy-
transition uncertainty. The thesis argues for a broader approach to reliability 
governance: one that clarifies the public-interest scope of reliability, embeds 
reliability alongside affordability and sustainability, and creates more collective 
mechanisms for managing uncertainty beyond technocratic institutions. 
 
Thesis supervisor: David Hsu 
Title: Associate Professor of Urban and Environmental Planning 
  

