Introduction  
Interconnection became a policy question when market restructuring separated 
generation from transmission. Beginning in the late 1990s, the U.S. electricity industry 
opened generation to competition while maintaining regulation over the transmission 
grid. Any new power plant must go through an interconnection process before 
connecting to the grid and transmitting electricity. This process involves engineering 
studies to ensure the new generator won't destabilize the system, overload 
transmission lines, cause voltage fluctuations, or disrupt power flow to millions of 
customers. It also includes cost studies to identify the necessary network upgrades to 
connect the resource to the grid. Regional Transmission Organizations (RTOs), which 
operate the grid across multiple states, manage the interconnection queues and 
establish rules under the oversight of the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission 
(FERC). 
The interconnection queues have been overwhelmed by the growing number of 
requests in recent years. As of 2024, more than 2,600 GW of proposed generation 
capacity is waiting in the queues across the United States, more than double the 
roughly 1,200 GW of generation installed on the grid.
1
 Approximately 12,000 projects 
were actively waiting. RTOs and FERC have responded with reforms aimed at thinning 
the queue and accelerating the process. RTOs adjusted from "first-come, first-served" 
to "first-ready, first-served" frameworks, requiring developers to demonstrate 
financial commitment and site control before entering or advancing in the queue.
2
 The 
Midcontinent Independent System Operator (MISO), serving the central U.S. and parts 
of the Gulf Coast, transitioned from a serial study—where projects were examined 
individually—to a cluster study in 2017, where groups of projects are analyzed together 
and their interconnection costs are allocated among them. PJM Interconnection (PJM), 
the largest grid operator in the country, serving the Mid-Atlantic and parts of the 
eastern U.S., tightened its requirements in 2019 to require site control and a financial 
commitment at the time of application. In 2022, a major queue reform package 
adopting a cluster study was approved under a FERC order, but the transition is 
 
