March 2025, and the Solar Energy Industries Association and Invenergy Renewables 
sued FERC in June 2025.
13
 ERAS went through a more circuitous process, first initiated 
in MISO's stakeholder process
14
 and then revised based on stakeholder input before 
MISO filed the proposal with FERC on March 17. FERC rejected it on May 16, 2025, in a 
2-1 decision with guidance for revision.
15
 MISO refiled on June 6, 2025, with a 68-
project total cap and a 10-per-quarter limit. FERC accepted the refiled proposal on 
June 21, 2025.
16
 After rehearing requests from multiple groups, Earthjustice, on behalf 
of a few environmental groups, filed a lawsuit challenging FERC’s approval of ERAS, 
referred to as “fast-track the interconnection of mostly fossil-fueled generation.”
17
 
The controversy reveals that queue-rationing policies are not simply technical 
responses to interconnection delay. They are decisions made under uncertainty: actors 
 
