 9 
dispute the severity of reliability needs, the causes of those needs, the harms of policy 
departures, and the likely outcomes of project selection. This thesis examines how 
uncertainties are managed in the making of rationing policies.   
The analysis draws on three sources of evidence. First, through docket analysis, I 
identify the uncertainties that entered the formal debate, how different stakeholders 
interpreted them, and how decision makers acted despite unresolved uncertainty. 
Second, through 18 interviews with clean energy advocates and analysts, I identify 
uncertainties that were marginalized or left outside the formal debate. Third, using 
queue composition data on ERAS and RRI, I examine the resource, ownership, and 
geographic patterns produced by rationing policies. Together, these analyses explain 
how uncertainty was introduced, addressed, narrowed, or omitted in the policy 
process, and how reliability urgency shaped the management of uncertainty. 
Interconnection governance faces pressures to modernize policymaking for the clean 
energy transition and to accommodate unprecedented load growth.  Researchers 
evaluate interconnection barriers using detailed queue metrics and interconnection 
cost analysis.
18
 Legal scholars examine siloed governance as an obstacle to clean 
energy and to holistic planning.
19
 Economists propose models for interconnection cost 
allocation and market design.
20
 The interconnection policy-making process remains 
understudied. This study contributes to that gap by empirically examining 
interconnection policy through case studies of RRI and ERAS, and by showing how 
uncertainty governance changes under conditions of reliability urgency. 
 
 
