Theory 
To understand uncertainty management and the impact of urgency in the policy 
process, I drew on theories for analytical aid. The Multiple Streams Approach (MSA) 
helps theorize how urgency reaches the agenda, as analyzed in the background. The 
Advocacy Coalition Framework (ACF) helps theorize how, once on the agenda, 
contests over uncertainty are organized and resolved among policy actors within 
institutions.   
Urgency in multiple stream approach  
The Multiple Streams Approach, originally developed by Kingdon,
21
 explains agenda-
setting and decision-making as the convergence of independently flowing streams 
under conditions of ambiguity.
22
 It contrasts with the rational policy process, which 
moves from problem identification, through exhaustive research and evaluation, to 
solution-finding. Problems, policy, and politics are treated as independent streams, 
aligned by policy entrepreneurs to reach the agenda and move their preferred package 
deals toward decisions. 
MSA theorizes the impact of urgency on policymaking through this stream model. Two 
fundamental assumptions are that time is a singular, irreplaceable resource and that 
attention is necessarily confined to a few issues on the agenda. Because decision-
makers' time is finite, policy windows open only briefly. Urgency further compresses 
the policy window. Zahariadis's "Plato's Receptacle" theorizes deadlines as temporal-
sorting mechanisms: shorter windows reduce conflict by limiting participation and 
intensifying framing effects, while externally imposed deadlines favor larger 
deviations from the status quo because policymakers can use them to deflect blame.
23
 
The "Shield of Heracles" adds an emotional endowment effect, in which novel, fear-
evoking events interrupt ongoing behavior and divert attention toward threat stimuli, 
enabling rapid coupling of problems and solutions.
24
 
 
