24
 Nikolaos Zahariadis, “The Shield of Heracles: Multiple Streams and the Emotional Endowment Effect.,” 
European Journal of Political Research 54, no. 3 (2015): 466–81, 108384587, https://doi.org/10.1111/1475-6765.12072. 

 11 
MSA also helps theorize the production of urgency. It does not deny the reality of 
urgent conditions but distinguishes between conditions and problems. Conditions are 
objective states of affairs; they become problems only when participants come to 
believe that something ought to be done to change them. This distinction contains 
what Herweg, Zahariadis, and Zohlnhöfer describe as a "perceptual, interpretive 
element": people define conditions as problems by comparing current circumstances 
with normative ideals, benchmarking against other jurisdictions, or categorizing a 
situation within a particular frame.
25
 Kingdon identifies three mechanisms through 
which conditions come to policymakers' attention. Indicators, for example, regularly 
published data on budgets, unemployment, energy prices, or system performance, 
measure the magnitude of conditions and make it easier for actors to frame them as 
problems requiring action. Focusing events, defined by Birkland as sudden and 
relatively rare, are at least potentially harmful and are known to policymakers and the 
public simultaneously, which can dramatically accelerate problem recognition.
26
 
Feedback from ongoing programs—like reports of failure, cost overruns, or 
unexpected consequences—serves as another way for conditions to become prominent 
in the problem stream.
27
 
Contestation in the advocacy coalition framework 
The Advocacy Coalition Framework (ACF) provides a baseline model, organized 
around the policy subsystem and its coalition actors that shape policymaking.
28
 As 
Paul Cairney observes, the ACF aims to make sense of complex policy systems, ranging 
from highly politicized disputes that draw broad participation to technical issues 
routinely handled outside public view.
29
 Decisions are typically produced under 
limited information and high levels of uncertainty and ambiguity. 
Sabatier characterizes the policy subsystem as an arena of "substantial goal conflicts, 
important technical disputes, and multiple actors from several levels of government." 
 
25
 Nicole Herweg et al., “Straightening the Three Streams: Theorising Extensions of the Multiple Streams 
Framework.,” European Journal of Political Research 54, no. 3 (2015): 435–49, 108384589, 
https://doi.org/10.1111/1475-6765.12089. 
26
 Thomas A. Birkland, After Disaster : Agenda Setting, Public Policy, and Focusing Events, in After Disaster : 
Agenda Setting, Public Policy, and Focusing Events, American Governance and Public Policy (Georgetown 
University Press, 1997). 
27
 Birkland, After Disaster : Agenda Setting, Public Policy, and Focusing Events. 
28
 Sabatier and Weible, Theories of the Policy Process. 
29
 Paul Cairney, “Paul A. Sabatier, ‘An Advocacy Coalition Framework of Policy Change and the Role of Policy-
Oriented Learning Therein,’” in The Oxford Handbook of Classics in Public Policy and Administration, ed. Martin 
Lodge et al. (Oxford University Press, 2015), https://doi.org/10.1093/oxfordhb/9780199646135.013.24. 

 12 
Within this arena operate advocacy coalitions — "people from a variety of positions 
(elected officials and agency officials, interest group leaders, researchers) who share a 
particular belief system — i.e., a set of basic values, causal assumptions, and problem 
perceptions — and who show a non-trivial degree of coordinated activity over time."
30
 
Typically, a dominant coalition—possessing greater resources and institutional 
influence—competes against one or more minority or challenger coalitions. This 
dominance endures through shaping policy narratives, controlling decision-making 
agendas, and maintaining unequal access to resources such as legal authority, public 
opinion, information, mobilizable constituents, financial backing, and effective 
leadership.
31
 
Venues are decision-making arenas in which coalitions attempt to influence the 
decisions made by other coalitions and by government agency officials. Venues are 
closely tied to subsystem types. Weible categorizes subsystems as unitary, adversarial, 
or collaborative. A unitary subsystem features a single dominant coalition, leading the 
final decision. An adversarial subsystem is marked by high conflict among competing 
coalitions, while a collaborative subsystem involves cooperative coalitions where 
conflict remains at intermediate levels. Traditional venues — legislative committees 
and subcommittees, courts, executives, and administrative agencies — tend to 
dominate adversarial and unitary subsystems and are limited in their capacity to 
resolve conflicts: courts, for example, settle procedural questions rather than 
substantive disputes, and these venues constrain participation among interested and 
affected actors and narrow discourse by relying largely on hearings and testimony. 
Alongside these traditional venues are consensus-based institutions, characterized by 
deliberative norms of engagement, inclusive participation, and transparent rules of 
negotiation, in which substantive issues can be discussed and potentially resolved. 
The choice and structure of venues, therefore, exerts substantial influence over policy 
outcomes.
32
 
 
30
 Paul A. Sabatier, “An Advocacy Coalition Framework of Policy Change and the Role of Policy-Oriented Learning 
Therein,” Policy Sciences 21, no. 2 (1988): 129–68, https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00136406. 
31
 Paul A. Sabatier and Hank C. Jenkins-Smith, Policy Change and Learning : An Advocacy Coalition Approach, in 
Policy Change and Learning : An Advocacy Coalition Approach, Theoretical Lenses on Public Policy (Westview 
Press, 1993). 
32
 Sabatier, “An Advocacy Coalition Framework of Policy Change and the Role of Policy-Oriented Learning 
Therein.” 

 13 
Uncertainty and expert-based information in the ACF 
Uncertainty in the policy subsystem is threefold: actors are unable to know the 
components that define and affect a subsystem (problem severity, the range of 
plausible causes and actions, and likely consequences); they are unable to fully 
measure or understand those components; and they are unable to know the links or 
probabilities between actions and outcomes.
33
 Such uncertainty affords discretion in 
interpretation, opening space for contestation.
34
  
Information is therefore central to the ACF's treatment of uncertainty. Weible 
distinguishes three uses of expert-based information: instrumental, political, and 
learning. The instrumental use rests on a rational, idealized model of problem solving 
— a problem is identified, research is conducted, and a decision follows from the 
findings. Instrumental use can reduce uncertainty under conditions of analytical 
tractability — Jenkins-Smith's concept clarifies when uncertainty is genuinely 
reducible by evidence rather than weaponized in political conflict. Instrumental use of 
science is most likely in professional forums where coalitions work cooperatively, 
often alongside scientists. The political use of information, by contrast, serves to 
mobilize coalition allies around an issue or to counter opponents' arguments during 
conflict; expert-based information thus becomes a coalition resource, and policy-
oriented learning tends to occur within a coalition rather than across coalitions. 
Learning across coalitions, in any case, is unlikely to occur over short periods.
35
 
Interconnection as a policy subsystem with coalitions, FERC as a venue 
I analyze the interconnection policy as a policy subsystem, defined as a decision-
making network of policy participants focused on policy issues within a geographic 
boundary. Coalitions in the interconnection policy subsystem include market 
participants, i.e., utilities, developers, power suppliers, and transmission owners, as 
well as purposive groups, including environmental groups, customer advocates 
representing consumer interests, and Regional Transmission Organizations as 
coordinators. 
 
