The condition: queue backlogs 
A project that sits in the queue for years is a common experience among developers. 
According to Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory(LBNL)'s utility-scale wind and 
solar survey, for projects completed between 2018 and 2023, measured from contract to 
commercial operation date, more than three-fourths of solar and wind projects took 
four to six years, and one-fifth took more than six years.
40
 In PJM, it now takes an 
average of about eight years to bring a new generator online.
 
For active projects still in 
the queue, estimated costs have grown eightfold since 2019.
 41
 MISO, despite 
implementing cluster studies and readiness requirements earlier, faces conditions that 
are no brighter. An experienced developer described one project without extensive 
transmission support: developed in 2019, it received approval only in 2025 and is 
planned to come online by 2027. From submission to GIA, six years in MISO is, in the 
developer's words, "lucky."
42
 Long waiting times and high interconnection costs are 
identified as the major barriers
43; uncertainties about policy changes and about other 
projects in the queue were raised by three developers I spoke with. 
Long waiting times result from several factors. One is the complex, time-intensive 
nature of studying the impact of a new generator on the regional grid. The standard 
sequence runs as follows: a project developer initiates a new interconnection request 
(IR), thereby entering the queue; a series of interconnection studies determines what 
new transmission equipment or upgrades may be needed and assigns the costs of that 
equipment; finally, an interconnection agreement (IA) — a contract between the ISO 
and the generation owner — stipulates operational terms and cost responsibilities. 
Interconnection studies can change when the queue status changes. When projects 
drop out, they create a domino effect in the cycle, causing subsequent projects to be 
re-studied. A second factor is the sheer number of requests and the growing 
interconnection queue. Across both MISO and PJM, the number of interconnection 
requests and the total capacity seeking access to the grid have surged. In 2020, the 
 
