 Will Gorman et al., “Grid Connection Barriers to Renewable Energy Deployment in the United States,” Joule 9, 
no. 2 (2025), https://doi.org/10.1016/j.joule.2024.11.008; Johnston et al., “An Empirical Analysis of the 
Interconnection Queue”; Dana Ammann, Waiting Game: How the Interconnection Queue Threatens Renewable 
Development in Pjm (Natural Resources Defense Council(NRDC), 2023), 
https://doi.org/10.1163/9789004322714_cclc_2023-0158-0738. 

 16 
total number of requests in the MISO queue was less than 200. In 2021, it gradually 
increased to around 500. In 2022, it surged to over 1,000 requests. MISO implemented 
some changes in 2023, but in 2024, requests rose again to over 600. PJM showed a 
similar trend, with even higher volumes. In 2021, it received over 1,500 requests and 
has since frozen new applications to clear the backlog.
 44
  
Withdrawals and high interconnection costs feed on each other, making 
interconnection difficult to manage. In MISO, between 2000 and 2020, 69% of the 2,782 
total requests were withdrawn on average, and 75% of 501 GW withdrew before signing 
an interconnection agreement. PJM's withdrawal rate is 70% across 5,590 requests. 
45
 
Network upgrade costs have been a major contributor to the high withdrawal rate. 
Under serial cost allocation, each generator pays for the transmission infrastructure 
needed when it connects to the grid. Often, one project is assigned a high cost to 
upgrade the network, but subsequent projects can then use the capacity created by 
the first project and are assigned a lower cost. When a project drops out, costs 
typically shift onto others, producing a domino effect of cancellations. Project 
developers, knowing there is a chance of getting lucky with a lower network upgrade 
cost assignment, have an incentive to enter multiple project proposals at multiple 
locations. Developers attempt to optimize their location strategies through 
prescreening, in-house modeling, and consultants. They are often uncertain about the 
outcome of interconnection studies because queue status changes — a previously 
entered project may be dropped after submission. These incentives and uncertainties 
produce a highly fluid queue. A technical professional described the impact of 
withdrawals on the queue: "No one raindrop is responsible for the flood. No single 
project is the problem. It's the collective volume."
46
 
Beyond the potential for speculation, the long wait time makes some projects no 
longer feasible. Developers described interconnection delays as a "pile-up"
47
 effect in 
which queue delays cascade into every other aspect of project development. Site land 
and permits typically expire in one or two years. Longer waiting times produce costly, 
wasteful cycles of redoing the permitting process. Equipment procurement and 
 
