 “Interview #14,” (Virtual meeting room), February 2026. 

 17 
financial closings are also delayed. These delays affect the financing of a private 
project and can render it no longer feasible. 
Beyond the potential for speculation, the long wait time makes some projects no 
longer feasible. Developers described interconnection delay as a "pile-up" effect in 
which queue delays cascade into every other aspect of project development. Site land 
and permits typically expire in one or two years. Longer waiting times produce costly, 
wasteful cycles of redoing the permitting process. Equipment procurement and 
financial closings are also delayed. All these delays affect the financing of a private 
project and can render it no longer feasible. 
Interconnection challenges have intensified with the advancement of clean 
technologies. The growth of renewables has added capacity and crowded out or 
replaced some old coal plants. Requests have also increased exponentially because 
clean-energy projects tend to be smaller than traditional, large fossil plants. In PJM, 
solar grew from 2009 to become the largest share in the queue
48
. Batteries also 
appeared in 2010 and have grown to roughly one-fourth of the occupied queue. MISO 
has seen a similar shift. The move from large, location-agnostic fossil-fuel plants to 
small-capacity renewables has increased the number of requests. The same 700 MW of 
capacity spread across ten or fifteen renewable energy sites — each in a different 
location with different grid conditions — is far more complex to study than a single 
concentrated site. Renewables, as intermittent resources, also require more advanced 
coordination technology, which has improved in recent years but operates on 
different time scales than fossil fuels. Clean energy is also locationally constrained and 
requires more transmission build-out to move power from rural generation sites to 
load centers.  
Transmission lines, however, have not progressed at a comparable pace. In 2023, only 
55 miles of new high-voltage regional transmission lines were built. The annual 
average of 1,700 miles of new high-voltage transmission from 2010 to 2014 dropped to 
925 miles from 2015 to 2019 and has fallen further to roughly 350 miles per year from 
2020 to 2023. Permitting, planning, and cost allocation, among other factors, shape 
overall transmission build-out, placing interconnection under physical constraints 
and making transmission capacity itself a temporarily scarce good. An analyst at the 
 
48
