 18 
Natural Resources Defense Council pointed to the underlying problem: an 
"increasingly maxed-out grid that has stagnated amid years of underinvestment in 
transmission infrastructure." 
49
 The problematic conditions exist in interconnection 
and outdated policies in the face of changing resources and the electricity market. The 
bottleneck, however, now faces new challenges. 
The challenge: unprecedented load  
After nearly two decades of essentially no more than 1% growth in electricity demand 
since the 2000s, the U.S. power system is absorbing a load shock, with data centers the 
single largest driver. Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory's December 2024 United 
States Data Center Energy Usage Report found that data-center electricity 
consumption more than doubled between 2018 and 2023, rising from roughly 76 TWh 
(1.9 % of U.S. electricity) to 176 TWh (4.4 %), and projected a 2028 range of 325 to 580 
TWh, or 6.7 to 12.0 % of national consumption
50
. The National Telecommunications and 
Information Administration counts more than 5,000 operating U.S. data centers and 
projects sectoral demand to grow by roughly 9% annually through 2030.
 51
  
In the PJM region, Northern Virginia's "Data Center Alley" in Loudoun County has been 
a central hub for data centers. 
52
 Loudoun County alone hosts more than 4,900 MW of 
operating data-center capacity. Dominion Energy's October 2024 Integrated Resource 
Plan, filed with the Virginia State Corporation Commission, projects that energy 
demand in its service territory will grow 5.5 % annually and more than double over the 
next fifteen years
53
, with data-center peak load reaching roughly 9 GW within a 
decade.
 54
 By the end of 2024, Dominion's contracted data-center capacity had grown 
from approximately 21 GW in July 2024 to 40 GW in December, an 88% increase over 
five months
55
. 
 
