The indicator: resource adequacy 
Resource adequacy is the concept used to assess whether sufficient generators and 
transmission capacity are available to meet demand. As one of three components of 
grid reliability in the bulk power system, it is defined as:  
“The ability of the electricity system to supply the aggregate electrical demand 
and energy requirements of the end-use customers at all times, taking into 
account scheduled and reasonably expected unscheduled outages of system 
elements.”
64
 
Resource adequacy is measured by the probability of undersupply. This probability 
can be managed by adding a planning reserve margin, an extra amount of capacity to 
account for uncertainty in load growth and outages of generators or transmission 
lines. Standard practice is a 15% reserve margin, which corresponds to a 1-in-10-year 
event requiring 15% more than the expected peak load.  
Multiple institutions with overlapping jurisdictions govern reliability and resource 
adequacy. FERC oversees bulk-power reliability through the approval and 
enforcement of mandatory reliability standards, while NERC, certified by FERC as the 
Electric Reliability Organization, develops reliability standards subject to FERC 
approval and oversight. These standards apply to registered users, owners, and 
operators of the bulk power system, including relevant grid operators. RTOs and ISOs 
also manage regional grid operations and implement FERC-approved market rules that 
shape resource adequacy. In MISO, resource adequacy is organized around seasonal 
capacity requirements; load-serving entities can demonstrate sufficient resources 
through self-supply, bilateral contracting, or market-based acquisition through 
MISO’s Planning Resource Auction. MISO’s auction functions mainly as a residual 
mechanism for entities with shortfalls. PJM, by contrast, relies more heavily on a 
centralized capacity market: its Reliability Pricing Model includes a Base Residual 
Auction and incremental auctions, and participation by load-serving entities is 
 
63
 D. Larson, Utility Experiences and Trends Regarding Data Centers: 2024 Survey (n.d.), accessed May 14, 2026, 
https://www.epri.com/research/products/000000003002030643. 
64
 National Renewable Energy Laboratory, Explained: Fundamentals of Power Grid Reliability and Clean Electricity, 
2024. 

 21 
mandatory unless they use the Fixed Resource Requirement alternative. States also 
play a major role through utility planning and procurement. More than 35 states 
require utilities to file integrated resource plans or equivalent planning documents, 
and IRPs are used to show how utilities plan to meet forecasted electricity demand 
over a future planning period.
65
 
Public statistics published by authoritative institutions highlight urgent reliability 
concerns. In May 2024, NERC published its long-term reliability assessment. In MISO, 
the expected reserve margin declines from 17% in 2025 to 10.6% in 2030. The NERC 
assessment indicates that the critical point will arrive in 2030. In PJM, the forecast 
reserve margin is 34.9% in 2025, declines to 23.1% in 2030, and falls further to 16.8% in 
2034 — below the reference margin level of 17.6%.  
The focusing event: spiking capacity prices 
Focusing events tend to direct attention to some problems and away from others.   As 
Cobb and Elder note, when legislators or government officials begin discussing a 
problem, the press tends to follow
66
. The capacity-market shock made the problem 
more tangible and visible and gave interconnection political salience once elected 
officials framed it as a crisis. 
The spike in PJM's capacity market made reliability concerns concrete and drew 
public attention. In 2024, auction prices in PJM cleared at $269.92/MW-day, compared 
with $28.92/MW-day the previous year. The capacity market does not pay for actual 
electricity; instead, plants are paid for being available to supply electricity in the 
future. In general economic terms, ample supply produces lower prices and scarcity 
produces higher prices. Prices in that auction were even higher in some regions: in the 
Baltimore Gas and Electric zone in Maryland, for example, prices hit the cap of 
$466.35/MW-day.
 67
 In the wake of the price spike, politicians increasingly discussed 
electricity supply. The U.S. Department of Energy declared an energy "emergency" in 
June when it ordered Constellation Energy to continue operating generating units in 
Pennsylvania that the company had planned to retire. In December 2024, Pennsylvania 
Governor Josh Shapiro described the auction outcome as "the largest unjust wealth 
 
