transfer in the history of US energy markets." The complaint Pennsylvania submitted 
to FERC against PJM cited three reasons for the unexpected outcome: significant load 
growth, the country's most congested interconnection queue, and a compressed 
capacity-auction schedule. PJM leadership has indicated that fixing the capacity 
market requires interconnection reforms: "The most enduring way to bring both 
capacity and energy market prices down is ensuring sufficient supply through 
functional interconnection queues." PJM leadership delivered the message at the 
Organization of PJM States meeting in October 2024.
68
 "I feel more concerned today 
than I did two years ago about resource adequacy," PJM CEO Manu Asthana said. 
"Load is growing much faster than we had projected then, which even back then was 
an eye-popping set of numbers," Asthana mentioned the Reliability Resource Initiative 
as a one-off effort to add generation capacity quickly.
69
 
 
Data and Method 
Docket documents include original tariffs filed by RTOs, comments submitted to FERC 
by various stakeholders, and the FERC order. I coded contested issues as uncertainties 
and stakeholders' interpretations. Interviews were selected to understand the context 
of interconnection governance and to explain uncertainties of concern that are not 
included in the formal record. Interviewees include clean-energy industry 
professionals, independent researchers, and advocacy organizations. Quantitative 
analysis of queue data provides evidence of queue composition in the fast-track and 
standard processes. 
Quantitative data on interconnection queues come from the Queued-Up dataset by 
Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory, covering project records from seven ISO/RTO 
queues in the U.S. through 2024. It includes project details like fuel type, capacity, 
entry date, and status. I used MISO and PJM subsets to analyze trends in requests, 
withdrawals, and fuel types. Additionally, I obtained ERAS and RRI project lists from 
 
68
 “PJM Capacity Auction Procures Sufficient Resources To Meet RTO Reliability Requirement | PJM Inside Lines,” 
accessed April 11, 2026, https://insidelines.pjm.com/pjm-capacity-auction-procures-sufficient-resources-to-
meet-rto-reliability-requirement/. 
69
 Manu Asthana, “Testimony of Manu Asthana,” U.S. HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES COMMITTEE ON ENERGY & 
COMMERCE, SUBCOMMITTEE ON ENERGY, March 25, 2025, https://www.pjm.com/-
/media/DotCom/library/reports-notices/testimony/2025/20250325-asthana-testimony-us-house-subcommittee-
on-energy.pdf. 

 23 
these websites to examine fuel, location, and capacity. The ERAS data also includes 
institutional info like customer, transmission owner, and project need summaries. 
I analyzed three FERC docket filings: Docket No. ER25-712 for PJM's RRI proposal; 
Docket No. ER25-1674 for MISO's initial ERAS filing; and Docket No. ER25-2454 for 
MISO's revised ERAS filing. For each proceeding, I reviewed the RTO's tariff revisions 
and the summaries in the FERC order. I then selected intervening comments for closer 
analysis from the full set of notices and responsive pleadings: 24 from the RRI docket, 
18 from the initial ERAS docket, and 36 from the revised ERAS docket. Filers included 
RTOs, utilities, independent power producers, state regulators, consumer advocates, 
and clean energy and environmental groups. I thematically analyzed these documents 
in two steps. First, I coded each document for contested issues related to uncertainty, 
identifying which uncertainties the filer raised, contested, or sought to resolve. 
Second, I organized the arguments and supporting evidence by institutional position, 
including the RTOs' explanations in their tariff revisions and motions for leave to 
answer, the responses from intervening stakeholders, and the reasoning FERC adopted 
in its orders. 
I conducted eighteen semi-structured interviews with clean-energy advocates, 
experts, and developers from MISO and PJM regions via video conference, each lasting 
30 minutes to an hour. Interviewees were chosen via three methods. First, I reached 
out to organizations that provided comments during the RTO stakeholder process or 
FERC comment period and contacted their representatives for interviews. Second, I 
approached individuals who signed petitions. Third, some participants were suggested 
by earlier interviewees. This process resulted in a sample of people actively involved 
in interconnection policy, many of whom participated directly in ERAS or RRI 
policymaking. 
Interviewees represent five organizational categories: environmental organizations, 
clean-energy trade associations, renewable-energy developers, consumer advocacy 
groups, and independent experts from consulting and academic research. To protect 
confidentiality, all interviewees are identified by number rather than name, and 
organizational affiliations are described at the category level. Nine interviewees were 
directly involved in the process, drafting comments at FERC. Four were actively 
engaged in stakeholder meetings and worked within active institutions, but did not 
sign off on public records. Five provide general expertise from working and 

 24 
researching on interconnection, with attention to fast-track policies. Eight 
interviewees engaged in both RTOs; six focused primarily on MISO and four on PJM. 
 
Table    1: Interview sample 
Each interview combined a standard set of questions with additional questions 
tailored to the interviewee's role and expertise. The standard questions covered three 
areas. The first concerned the interviewee's engagement in interconnection policy: 
how they and their organization became involved, why organizations chose to form or 
join coalitions, and how those coalitions were organized. The second addressed their 
experience with RTO stakeholder processes and how the ERAS and RRI proceedings 
compared with engagement on other interconnection reforms. The third, and central, 
focus was the uncertainties in ERAS and RRI, including the targeted, the potential 
harm, and the possible outcome. Within this third area, interviewees were also invited 
to discuss how to manage those uncertainties. Two open-ended questions closed each 
interview: what alternatives to fast-track approaches the interviewee would have 
preferred, and what role long-term transmission planning should play in 
interconnection reform. For developers, questions focused on past interconnection 
reforms and on decisions to participate in ERAS or RRI. For interviewees with policy 
and strategy expertise, additional emphasis was placed on governance challenges and 
the dynamics of stakeholder engagement. For those with technical backgrounds, 
questions addressed the conduct of interconnection studies, models and data, and the 
design criteria for fast-track policies. For interviewees with legal expertise, additional 
questions covered the division of state and federal jurisdiction, legal precedent, 
litigation strategies, and the legal burden of advocating for or protesting such policies. 
 

 25 
Docket: contested uncertainties within the federal regulatory venue 
Under conditions of reliability urgency, RTOs, under pressure to reduce 
interconnection delays, suggested queue-rationing as targeted interventions. In the 
ERAS and RRI dockets at FERC, the resulting controversy centered on uncertainties 
within the interconnection policy subsystem. First, coalitions disputed the 
components defining the problem: the severity and geographic specificity of the 
reliability concern, the extent to which that concern could be clearly identified, and 
the necessity of queue rationing as the appropriate policy response. Second, they 
disagreed over whether key components could be measured or understood with 
sufficient confidence and acknowledged that cost impacts and system consequences 
were uncertain but were interpreted differently across coalitions. Third, they 
contested the assumed link between policy design and outcomes, particularly whether 
viability criteria could reliably identify resources capable of delivering the promised 
reliability benefits. 
Uncertainty over reliability needs  
Rationing policies were based on short-term reliability needs, but uncertainties about 
the magnitude and timing left the shortfall disputed. Utilities largely accepted the 
forecasts; clean energy actors pushed back, urging that a clearly defined reliability 
need serve as the threshold for ERAS and RRI. 
PJM’s reliability needs estimate. In the proposed RRI tariff, PJM quantified the "acute 
need" as a 10-Gigawatt capacity shortfall for the 2030/31 delivery year, and this figure 
"could potentially swell to 26 Gigawatts once the most recent load forecast is 
finalized."
70
 The 10 Gigawatts originated in PJM's internal report, Resource 
Retirements, Replacements & Risks, also known as the "Four Rs" report, published in 
February 2023.
71
 The Four Rs report explained the shortfall using a balance sheet that 
compared generation resources’ supply with the near-term load forecast. The supply 
side accounts for planned retirements; new entries from gas, renewables, and storage; 
and the declining capacity contribution of intermittent resources as they take a larger 
share of the mix. Existing supply decreases due to expected retirements driven by 
state and federal policies and deteriorating economics. PJM assumed that new supply 
 
