alternative measures to meet demand. PJM had applied a single historical completion 
rate across all projects in the queue, regardless of whether they had executed 
interconnection agreements. Sierra Club and Appalachian Voices called this outdated: 
"PJM's supply conditions have also changed since early 2023...PJM does, in fact, have 
other means at its disposal for bringing new generation online to meet 2030 demand."
83
  
Clean energy proposed alternative modeling. In an affidavit submitted with Invenergy 
Renewables LLC 's protest, experts from the Rocky Mountain Institute pointed to gaps 
in PJM's modeling that missed recent efforts to increase supply and several ongoing 
policy reforms. PJM's projection had not accounted for Surplus Interconnection 
Service, filed just before the RRI with an effective date of March 7, 2025; pending 
generator-replacement rules allowing new resources to use existing grid headroom; 
and broader enhancements to completion rates across the Fast Lane and Transition 
Cycle. Once these were factored in, the experts concluded that "these results indicate 
that the RRI is not the only option for averting a supply shortfall and in fact may not 
be necessary."
84
 
MISO stacked reports for urgency. In response to forecasting critiques, Andrew 
Witmeier, MISO's Director of Resource Utilization, attached Q&A-style testimony to 
the revised second ERAS proposal citing six reports, both MISO and non-MISO, all 
pointing to growing challenges in meeting state energy goals within the MISO 
footprint. The 2024 MISO Regional Resource Assessment (RRA) projected a capacity 
 
