Uncertainty over cost shifting 
Because the two rationing policies operated as different mechanisms from the 
standard queue, their impact on existing projects was uncertain, and the scale of that 
impact was heavily disputed by developers and interconnection customers. The 
technical record produced a paradox. Both PJM's joint-study approach and MISO's 
separate-study approach were defended by the proposing RTO as protective of the 
existing queue and attacked by protesters as harmful to it. Each side cited engineering 
expertise and produced quantified results, yet the conclusions ran in opposite 
directions. Hess further noted that the concrete modeling details in ERAS were not 
specified in the Tariff and had only surfaced in stakeholder meetings before the FERC 
filing. 
PJM saw a limited cost-shifting impact. RRI is a one-time addition of 51 projects to the 
ongoing study cycle (Transition Cycle 2), where they will be studied in clusters 
alongside existing projects that applied before September 30, 2021. PJM reasoned that 
a 50-project cap would not delay Transition Cycle 2 and would have a limited impact 
on existing projects, since RRI is designed to select projects that are easier to study, 
thereby reducing study delays. As for network upgrade costs, PJM contended the 
impact on existing projects would be limited and potentially favorable: RRI projects 
are less likely to cause reliability violations, and therefore less likely to require 
network upgrades in the first place. PJM further anticipated that RRI could 
"contribute to the need for multiple network upgrades already needed for Transition 
Cycle 2, thereby spreading the cost allocation for such upgrades more broadly and 
relieving the legacy projects from some of the responsibility."
92
 At the time of filing, 
 
