94
 Invenergy Renewables submitted modeling estimating "over $8 billion in 
additional network upgrade costs caused by adding 20 GWs of RRI projects," in an 
affidavit from an experienced industry professional.
95
 
MISO saw cost isolation in ERAS through separate queue. ERAS runs quarterly from 
2025 through its 2028 sunset, processing up to 10 projects per quarter and 68 in total, 
each studied individually. A $100,000 application fee from ERAS applicants funds the 
additional work, including improved study tools and new staff, so that "no resource or 
staff time will be diverted from the Definitive Planning Phases (DPP, MISO's standard 
queue) to ERAS or vice versa." The quarterly cap also gives MISO room to manage 
coordination. To prevent the two tracks from competing for the same grid capacity, 
MISO runs ERAS and DPP studies on the same underlying model of the transmission 
system (the MTEP model): "Any headroom used by an ERAS interconnection request is 
not deducted from the DPP model, as ERAS interconnection request will not be 
included in ongoing DPP projects."
96
 MISO acknowledged a risk in studying the same 
physical grid through two separate processes, namely that transmission lines could 
end up oversubscribed, with more projects assigned to them than they can carry. Its 
 
