Uncertainty over viability criteria 
Rationing policies established selection criteria that filter out projects with higher 
capacity contributions and a higher likelihood of quick construction. However, actors 
questioned the criteria's ability to select the most viable projects among diverse 
possibilities.  
PJM defined shovel-ready. RRI is positioned to "enable 'shovel-ready' projects 
addressing near-term resource adequacy and/or reliability needs to be completed in a 
short period of time." PJM defined "shovel-ready" as resources that, first, "are ready to 
commence and complete construction quickly" and, second, "would offer sizeable 
amounts of Unforced Capacity with higher ELCC ratings."
99
 PJM's scoring system 
reflects these two priorities: 35 points for commercial viability and 65 points for 
market impact. Commercial viability covers commercial operation date, project 
support, RRI uprates, and headroom. Market impact covers unforced capacity, 
Effective Load Carrying Capability (ELCC, which measures an individual resource's 
contribution to capacity by discounting nameplate capacity, especially for 
renewables), and location. Through this balanced rating, PJM sees RRI as seeking "a 
 
97
 MISO Independent Power Producers, Protest of the Miso Independent Power Producers, Docket No.ER25-2454-
000 (2025). 
98
 NextEra Energy Resources, LLC, Motion to Intervene and Protest of NextEra Energy Resources, LLC. 
99
 PJM Interconnection, L.L.C., Tariff Revisions for Reliability Resource Initiative. 

 32 
diverse set of resources with differing operational characteristics and different fuel 
types." 
Critics doubted that RRI's scoring rules could select viable projects. The Natural 
Resources Defense Council, among the environmental groups protesting RRI, called 
the commercial-readiness criteria "not robust."
100
 The most important attribute, the 
commercial operation date (when plants can come online), weighs only 10 points, and 
"non-viable projects could be selected." Power suppliers raised the same concern with 
concrete numbers. AES Corporation gave an illustrative example in its protest: 
assuming the same location, a 1,000 MW nuclear uprate with no permits, an expected 
operation date of September 2030, and no available headroom would receive a high 
market-impact score but a lower commercial-viability score, totaling 85 points; a 1,000 
MW four-hour storage facility with all approved permits, available headroom, and a 
2028 in-service date would score only 70.75 points, because its lower capacity value 
reduces its market-impact score. The project that could come online sooner would 
rank lower under RRI's scoring. And, AES Corporation argued, plants entering service 
in 2030 would not address the near-term need.
101
 
The weighting drew objections even from RRI's supporters. American Municipal Power 
supported the concept behind RRI but argued that "eligibility criteria are 
inappropriately weighted toward UCAP and ELCC over in-service date viability and 
location,"
102
 and proposed location and timing as screening criteria rather than minor 
scoring factors. The Independent Market Monitor warned that under the proposed 
scoring, a project could score poorly on commercial viability but still be selected if it 
had high-capacity values. The IMM's position was that no project should be approved 
unless it first met strict thresholds for location, operational characteristics, and 
commercial viability.
103
 
Beyond the weighting itself, some protesters argued the scoring could not capture 
what was really holding projects back. Roughly 38,000 MW of resources had already 
cleared PJM's interconnection queue but remained unbuilt, held up by financing, 
 
