growth is the inability to predict when or where they will pop up. It is important to 
note too that MISO is not trying to solve a problem with consequences being felt 
today but is proposing a process that will allow it to address resource adequacy 
and reliability problems that will occur in the future to prevent foreseeable 
shortfalls.”
114
 
The management of this uncertainty followed established practice. "Historically, FERC 
has been careful not to second-guess the reliability determinations made by RTOs," 
noted an affidavit from the Electric Power Supply Association in support of RRI. The 
Commission ruled that PJM has the authority to manage its queue to meet projected 
near-term resource adequacy needs, citing PJM's responsibility to "ensure that there 
are enough idle generators connected to the transmission grid for the system to 
function at peak load," and reaffirming that it accords "reliability-related discretion to 
[ISOs], and [will] not second-guess their decisions in that regard."
 115
 On ERAS, the 
Commission concluded: "While forecasting future resource adequacy needs 
necessarily involves uncertainty, we find that it is reasonable for MISO to act in 
recognition of the aforementioned reports.”
116
 
FERC accepted the uncertainty with capping the policies as temporary, one-time 
measures. The potential harm remained unknown, as the Commission acknowledged 
in its order: 
"We also agree with PJM that there is no way to definitively know in advance the 
cost allocation impact from the RRI projects... Furthermore, as PJM has argued, 
efforts to definitively ascertain and allocate the exact RRI cost allocation impacts 
would take an additional nine months, which would undermine the purpose of the 
RRI reforms."
117
 
Though the impact is partially unknown, FERC concluded that the RRI-induced cost 
shifts raised by protesters were "speculative." It declined to engage with cost shifting 
in ERAS for similar reasons, treating it as a structural feature of the system: 
 
114
 David Rosner et al., 192 FERC ¶ 61,064 UNITED STATES OF AMERICA FEDERAL ENERGY REGULATORY 
COMMISSION, n.d. 
115
 Phillips et al., 190 FERC ¶ 61,084 UNITED STATES OF AMERICA FEDERAL ENERGY REGULATORY COMMISSION. 
116
 David Rosner and Lindsay S. See, 191 FERC ¶ 61,131 UNITED STATES OF AMERICA FEDERAL ENERGY 
REGULATORY COMMISSION, n.d. 
117
 Phillips et al., 190 FERC ¶ 61,084 UNITED STATES OF AMERICA FEDERAL ENERGY REGULATORY COMMISSION. 

 36 
interconnection customers possess "no legal rights to a given system topology," and 
lower-queued projects frequently face changing costs under any queue design. 
Beyond the order, commissioners shared more concerns about dismissing the cost-
shifting uncertainty. Commissioners Phillips and Rosner concurred in the RRI filing: 
"This is not a request we take lightly. ... threatens to seriously undermine 
confidence in Commission-jurisdictional electricity markets. Nonetheless, we 
believe that the Commission must confront the real and consequential harm that 
failing to act could have on consumers in the PJM region."
118
 
In their view, the consequences of failing to meet reliability needs ultimately 
outweighed the potential harm, given the bounded design of "a one-time stop gap 
measure" and PJM's pursuit of "additional reforms to ensure that PJM never needs a 
mechanism like RRI ever again." Commissioner Chang disagreed with that trade-off. 
She was not persuaded that PJM's proposal was "adequately tailored" to its stated 
goals, particularly given the harms it would likely inflict on other interconnection 
customers, and did not find those harms "mitigated to the extent possible." She drew a 
line between acceptable and unacceptable departures from standard interconnection 
practice: departures for surplus interconnection service or retirement-facility 
replacement, she wrote, "may warrant a departure from otherwise applicable open 
access requirements" because of clear efficiency gains. RRI, in her view, had not met 
that bar. 
FERC reviewed both designs under a 'just and reasonable' standard, asking whether 
they were defensible rather than optimal. The commissioners acknowledged 
uncertainty about whether the design would deliver shovel-ready resources. 
Commissioners Phillips and Rosner, concurring, voiced reservations, concerned that 
"PJM's proposal may not enable sufficient 'shovel-ready' resources to interconnect... 
to prevent the resource adequacy crisis that motivated PJM to develop this proposal in 
the first place." Commissioner Chang, in dissent, similarly questioned the heavy 
weight on capacity size and its links to external delay: larger facilities would get 
priority, but their greater complexity would likely delay development and 
construction.
 
 
118
 Phillips et al., 190 FERC ¶ 61,084 UNITED STATES OF AMERICA FEDERAL ENERGY REGULATORY COMMISSION. 

 37 
Despite this uncertainty, the Commission found PJM's scoring "reasonable" because 
the higher weight on capacity size aligned with the goal of lowering the capacity 
market price. Whether the weighting was optimal, FERC deferred to PJM: 
"PJM does not need to establish that the proposed criteria are ideal weighting 
criteria. Rather, PJM must simply establish that the proposed criteria represent a 
just and reasonable approach. Because we find PJM's proposal appropriately 
evaluates which resources will meet its near-term system needs, as discussed 
below, we do not need to evaluate whether alternative scoring methods would be 
reasonable than PJM's proposal."
 119 
FERC described MISO's viability requirements as a "comprehensive package" that 
would "deter speculative interconnection requests."
120
 It especially endorsed state 
certification as a tool outside the interconnection process for managing permitting 
uncertainties, finding it reasonable to rely on RERRA verification to identify projects 
that could most readily meet regional needs. RERRAs, the Commission wrote, are 
"obliged to serve the public interest in accordance with state law" and are "uniquely 
positioned" to evaluate which projects can best meet their region's reliability needs. 
Summary  
The docket analysis finds that the contested uncertainties were rooted in complex 
system issues beyond rationing policies. In the decision-making process under a 
pressing timeline and the existing structure of RTO-FERC governance, uncertainties 
are managed by deterring dominant coalitions and accepting temporary, suboptimal 
measures rather than by further specification through sufficient engagement. 
Uncertainties contested as system issues. Uncertainty over forecasting reflects not 
only technical error but also broader governance challenges. On the demand side, 
data-center load growth raised questions about how load requests are collected and 
verified. Utilities may have incentives to treat prospective data centers as future load, 
while limited coordination can allow the same request to be counted multiple times as 
developers shop across service territories. On the supply side, RTO scenarios often 
rely on historical trends, while clean energy researchers argued that ongoing policy 
efforts, including surplus interconnection service, could add capacity that models 
 
119
 Phillips et al., 190 FERC ¶ 61,084 UNITED STATES OF AMERICA FEDERAL ENERGY REGULATORY COMMISSION. 
120
 Rosner et al., 192 FERC ¶ 61,064 UNITED STATES OF AMERICA FEDERAL ENERGY REGULATORY COMMISSION. 

 38 
should capture. These disputes show that reliability forecasts depend on choices 
about data collection, scenario assumptions, and the representation of uncertainty.  
Uncertainty over cost shifting as a potential harm reflects structural problems in 
existing cost-allocation methods. Network upgrade costs are studied serially or in 
clusters, with assigned projects paying for upgrades that may also benefit later 
generators. Because cost responsibility shifts with queue position, rationing policies 
can redistribute costs whether they inject projects into an existing cycle, as in RRI, or 
create a separate track, as in ERAS. These cost shifts are difficult to model within a 
policy proceeding, making their distribution uncertain. This points to two suggestions: 
more holistic transmission planning to address the free-rider problem over the long 
term, and greater near-term transparency in models and studies to align expert 
interpretation. The harm-benefit trade-off intensified the dispute: system-level 
benefits remained uncertain, project-level benefits accrued to fast-tracked projects, 
and potential harms fell on projects already positioned in the existing queue. This 
uneven distribution of gains and losses helps explain why queue-rationing policies 
became controversial. 
Uncertainty over outcomes concerns whether selection criteria could identify projects 
best positioned to meet reliability needs. Projects were evaluated through indicators 
of capacity contribution or viability, but these indicators could not fully account for 
external factors that might delay or prevent completion. The criteria relied on 
capacity-accreditation measures such as Unforced Capacity and Effective Load 
Carrying Capability, whose application across resource types remains contested. They 
also involved institutional design: ERAS’s state regulatory notification requirement 
introduced state judgment into interconnection governance, but without fully 
resolving how need, suitability, and project priority should be determined. This 
suggests that selection criteria require both updated technical measures and clearer 
institutional roles. 
Uncertainties are resolved through venue rules under reliability urgency. FERC 
ultimately resolved these uncertainties under the existing rules. The Commission 
deferred technical specifics to the RTOs, accepting forecasting uncertainties and 
criteria design as within RTO discretion. Reliability urgency pressed the timeline 
against further inquiry: there was no time for detailed modeling to reduce forecast 
uncertainty, no time to wait for more accurate forecasts as the situation demanded 

 39 
immediate action, and no time for back-and-forth on what would be optimal rather 
than what had been presented. 
 
Interview: uncertainties beyond the formal debate 
Docket analysis shows how actors contested uncertainty about reliability needs, 
potential harm, and possible outcomes. Interviews with clean energy advocates and 
experts reveal additional uncertainties that largely remained outside the formal 
debate. At FERC, the reliability need was debated mainly in terms of forecasting 
accuracy, while interviewees questioned whether all forms of demand should be 
incorporated into the concept of reliability. Potential harm was discussed mainly in 
relation to queued projects, while interviewees emphasized the broader risk of 
repeated procedural departures under reliability pressure. Possible outcomes were 
debated through project-viability criteria, while interviewees raised the 
environmental consequences of the resource mix those criteria could advance. The 
interviews, therefore, show that the formal debate narrowed which uncertainties were 
institutionally visible. 
Hyperscalers as uncertain reliability need 
Some interviewees suspect that the urgent demand for increased data center capacity, 
along with utilities’ lobbying within RTOs, was a major force behind the advancement 
of queue-rationing policies.
121
 Data centers tend to be much less sensitive to price 
changes than standard industrial loads, fostering a "rush mentality"
122
 focused on 
speeding up interconnection processes. Additionally, utilities appear motivated to 
attract large customers.
123
 Large tech companies and data centers possess the financial 
resources and political influence to advocate for rapid policy changes. As an attorney 
at Earthjustice noted:  
“The interconnection process has consistently been a barrier...It’s only now that 
we see this need from data centers and big tech that we’re seeing all these new 
proposals to fix the interconnection process, at least for certain resources, for 
 
