February 2026; “Interview #11,” (Virtual meeting room), February 2026. 
122
 “Interview #17.” 
123
 “Interview #1.” 

 40 
certain types of customers... I think that there’s just something that does not pass 
the sniff test with these interconnections fast-tracks when you zoom out.”
124
 
Similarly, A clean energy analyst commented:  
“I think that [ERAS] is a symptom of wanting to have something in place so that 
you can get these new load additions online as soon as possible. This speed-to-
power thing for data center developers, specifically industry pressure.”
125
  
It was unknown whether RRI and ERAS were explicitly mobilized to meet the needs of 
hyperscale data centers. But interviews commonly indicate that data center load 
growth reduced resource adequacy, prompting RTOs to act quickly by rationing the 
interconnection queue.  
However, they question whether the reliability scope should include data centers, in 
addition to other loads. RWE Clean Energy stated in their protest, “If there is a new 
increase in data center energy needs, that is not a reliability problem,” indicating that 
data centers can be connected later when the market is ready. Although this question 
of the scope of reliability appeared only briefly in the docket comments, it echoed 
views expressed more fully in interviews. As a policy analyst at a clean energy trade 
association put it, “Speed to power is not a reliability issue but a business problem.”
126
 
A clean energy advocate in a MISO state said: 
“Utilities sometimes have a statutory obligation to serve... it feels like we are 
bending over backwards to get the energy for these sometimes speculative 
projects as fast as possible, and throwing the reliable, affordable, sustainable 
goals out the window for that...” 
Interviewees cautioned against directly treating hyperscale data-center demand as a 
reliability problem. In their view, policymakers should scrutinize hyperscale load 
requirements more carefully rather than rushing to prioritize large-capacity additions 
regardless of cost. 
Recurring stopgaps as uncertain harm 
Aside from the legal debate over whether rationing policies are exceptions to the 
open-access principle, these measures are described as temporary and one-off to 
 
124
 “Interview #6.” 
125
 “Interview #3.” 
126
 “Interview #13,” (Virtual meeting room), February 2026. 

 41 
justify deviations from standard practice. However, interviewees doubt this framing 
will last, fearing that rationing may not remain an exception but instead become the 
norm, replacing the ongoing reforms aimed at broader interconnection. 
Five legal professionals raised a version of the "exception swallows the rule" problem: 
rather than prompting a comprehensive fix, rationing policies may proliferate into a 
series of narrow, surgical workarounds.
127
 An experienced attorney at a trade 
association doubted the one-time framing would hold, observing that “the more that 
the load growth figures keep looking really high, and the more that grid operators 
believe that FERC is going to approve any expedited interconnection approach that 
they put forward, it certainly runs the risk of it coming back again.”
128
 
PJM's Expedited Interconnection Track (EIT), filed with FERC in February 2026, 
illustrates this concern. Similar in design to ERAS, EIT would use a serial study process 
to review projects on a first-come, first-served basis at regular intervals, with a 
minimum Unforced Capacity of 250 MW. “  One-offs keep coming,” a policy analyst 
commented on EIT, pointing to a joint letter from New Jersey Governor Mikie Sherrill 
and Illinois Governor JB Pritzker to PJM that signaled states' emerging concerns about 
rationing policies: 
“Setting a minimum eligibility requirement of 250 MW Unforced Capacity does not 
align with the intended goal of fixing the near-term capacity crisis and limits the 
ability of the EIT to relieve resource adequacy concerns, to improve affordability, 
and to support economic development.”
129
 
The deeper concern is that this pattern undermines FERC's broader 2023 effort to 
modernize interconnection. Another attorney described rationing policies as a 
“piecemeal patchwork” that undermines ongoing reforms designed to study small 
resources in clusters — reforms intended to improve efficiency and accommodate the 
paradigm shift in the resource mix. ERAS, in this view, “retrench[es] into studying 
singular, large resources, one at a time.”
130
 
 
