 42 
Environmental consequences as uncertain outcomes 
The formal debate over outcome uncertainty focused on whether rationing criteria 
could identify projects likely to deliver reliability benefits. Interviews raised a 
different uncertainty that the docket largely excluded: the environmental 
consequences of the resource mix those criteria were likely to advance. Several 
interviewees expressed confidence that, in practice, the criteria would favor large gas 
projects. One advocate said professionals can "read the tea leaves” and it's “very likely 
that Louisiana will expect large-scale gas projects”.
131
 Similarly, a renewables 
developer said simply, "We know those policies are not for us.”
132
 If rationed queues 
are expected to advance primarily gas resources, then environmental and health 
impacts become part of the policy’s likely outcomes, even if they are not treated as 
central in the formal proceeding. As one analyst explained, the debate had been 
narrowed in a way that excluded these impacts, leaving environmental organizations 
to bring them back to the table.
133
 
These concerns also intersect with state clean energy goals. Many states in the PJM 
and MISO regions have enacted clean energy targets. Virginia’s Clean Economy Act 
requires Dominion Energy to deliver 100 percent clean electricity by 2045 and 
Appalachian Power to do so by 2050. Illinois’s Climate and Equitable Jobs Act 
establishes a state policy of achieving 100 percent clean energy by 2050 and phasing 
out private gas-fired generation by 2045. Several interviewees questioned whether 
rationing policies that advance anticipated gas generation would undermine these 
targets. An attorney at the Southern Environmental Law Center working on Virginia 
issues described data-center-driven load growth as pressuring the construction of 
large fossil-fueled generators, which she characterized as “a threat to the targets set 
by the state.”
134
 If a fast-track interconnection pathway preferentially advances gas 
projects, she argued, interconnection outcomes will determine whether state clean 
energy targets remain on track. An analyst working in the PJM region noted that the 
gas projects selected through RRI, combined with continued development of rationing 
pathways, raise concerns about state clean energy goals.
135 
 
 
131
