 43 
The consequences become more significant over the long term. Gas projects advanced 
through current rationing policies may operate for 25 to 40 years, meaning that short-
term reliability responses can commit the grid to fossil-fuel infrastructure for decades. 
A policy analyst at the Southern Renewable Trade Association noted that if state and 
federal grids adhere to clean energy targets, gas plants built today will not operate at 
high-capacity factors over their economic lives. However, gas is what the market 
currently demands, and political pressure to bring new load online quickly favors 
building what can be built fastest. The long-term consequences are underprioritized 
amid reliability pressures.  
 
Rationed queues: uncertainties reflected in queue composition 
RRI and ERAS both produced gas-heavy queues, in contrast to the standard 
interconnection queues, where solar, wind, and storage dominate. The scale of gas in 
the rationed queues is substantial relative to each region's existing gas capacity. In 
PJM, existing installed gas capacity is 104.3 GW, while RRI includes 8.1 GW of gas 
projects. In MISO, existing installed gas capacity is 81 GW, while ERAS includes 20.7 
GW of gas projects.
136
 Although these rationing policies are bounded by sunset dates, 
eligibility limits, and project caps, their effects could still be significant if the selected 
projects are realized. 
ERAS also shows how rationing criteria can shape not only resource type, but also 
market structure and geography. Projects serving data centers account for nearly 43 
percent of total ERAS capacity, while utility-sponsored projects account for 39 
percent. Concentration is also high among interconnection customers: parent 
companies of the top five customers account for 80.9 percent of projects. State-level 
variation is also evident, since ERAS eligibility incorporates state preferences or 
certifications into the selection process. Together, these patterns suggest that queue 
rationing can produce a distinct queue composition, with implications for fuel mix, 
customer concentration, and state clean energy goals. 
 
136
