 44 
Gas dominates in the RRI queue  
RRI selection results were published in February 2025. 97 projects applied; 51 were 
selected, for a combined 11,793 MW, roughly one-third of the new capacity that 
entered PJM's standard cluster queue before 2022, the last year before PJM paused 
new queue entries. Among selected projects, gas accounted for 8.1 GW (69.2% of 
selected capacity), battery for 19.3%, and nuclear for 11.7%. Over a decade-long 
trajectory in PJM’s queued capacity, gas dominated PJM's queue through the mid-
2010s. From roughly 2015 onward, the share of gas in new queue entries declined 
steadily, and by 2020, new entries were predominantly solar, wind, and storage. RRI's 
69% gas share is the first time since 2016 that gas has accounted for most of the queue 
capacity. 
 
Figure    1: Queued capacity fuel mix in PJM, standard queue (2010–2023) vs. RRI (2025). 
Data source: Reliability Resource Initiative Summary, PJM Interconnection, LLC  ; Queued up, LBNL     
 
Selection outcomes varied by resource type. The selection process shows disparities 
across resource types. Of 53 gas projects that applied, 38 were selected, yielding a 66% 
approval rate. Of 32 battery storage projects that applied, 5 were selected, as shown in 
Figure 2.  
 

 45 
 
Figure    2: Queued projects by fuel mix in selected vs. unselected RRI process 
Data source: Reliability Resource Initiative Summary, PJM Interconnection, LLC 
 
Gas dominates in the ERAS queue  
ERAS took effect in August 2025 and is planned to run quarterly for three years, with 
each cycle capped at 10 projects. Because ERAS is eligibility-based, projects will be 
studied in the order eligibility is secured. Applications flooded in as soon as the 
application window opened. Within the first minute, 36 projects applied, filling the 
first two study cycles. By the end of March 2026, 61 projects had applied. The first 
cycle has completed its study, and post-GIA construction has yet to start. 8 projects 
withdrew.  
Gas accounts for 20.7 GW of ERAS queued capacity, or 73% of the total. Storage is 
12.9%, solar 8.9%, wind 3.3%, and nuclear 2.3%. This composition departs from the 
trajectory of MISO's standard cluster queue, where gas share in new entries has 
declined since the mid-2010s and recent annual cohorts have been predominantly 
solar, wind, and storage. 
The scale of individual ERAS gas projects is also distinct from the standard queue. 
Among the 30 gas projects in ERAS, 8 exceed 1,000 MW. For comparison, MISO's 
standard cluster queue contained 26 gas projects above 1,000 MW across the 24 years 

 46 
from 2000 through 2024. ERAS has produced roughly one-third of that 24-year count in 
its first seven months. 
 
Figure    3: Queued capacity fuel mix in MISO, standard queue (2010–2024) vs. ERAS (2025).
 
Data source: ERAS Interconnection Request, Midcontinent Independent System Operator, Inc.; Queued up, LBNL  
 
Developer, load type and state concentration in ERAS 
The five largest interconnection customers in ERAS submitted a total of 34 projects, 
which make up 80.9% of the overall queued capacity. Entergy Corporation was the top 
filer, submitting projects via its Louisiana, Mississippi, and Texas subsidiaries. They 
were followed by NiSource, Alliant Energy, NextEra Energy, and Invenergy. Among 
these five, three are vertically integrated load-serving entities, while two are 
independent developers. 
 
Table    2: Projects by top 5 parent companies of interconnection customers in ERAS queue 
Data source: ERAS Interconnection Request, Midcontinent Independent System Operator, Inc 

 47 
 
18 of 61 ERAS projects involve a utility interconnecting to its own transmission system. 
These projects represent 39% of queued capacity. Under the open-access framework 
that governs U.S. bulk power interconnection, the interconnection customer and the 
transmission owner are typically arm's-length parties; the study process is designed to 
enforce nondiscriminatory access. When the two are the same corporate entity, cost 
allocation, scheduling, and study assumptions become decisions made within a single 
firm. This practice is permitted. Its prevalence in ERAS, at 30% of project count and 
39% of capacity, is higher than in MISO's standard cluster queue. Entergy's subsidiaries 
alone account for 9 self-interconnecting projects totaling 8,660 MW. All are gas-fired. 
Individual unit sizes range from 377 MW to 1,640 MW. The largest single 
interconnection service request in ERAS, at over 2,000 MW, comes from Northern 
Indiana Public Service Company (NIPSCO). The project is two combined-cycle gas 
turbines at NIPSCO's Schahfer site. Schahfer's coal-fired units had been scheduled for 
retirement in 2025. In 2025, the U.S. Department of Energy ordered the coal units to 
continue operating through the end of the year. NIPSCO's 2024 Integrated Resource 
Plan discloses 1,285 MW of new combined-cycle gas additions for 2025 to 2029, planned 
only if new hyperscaler data center load is contracted.
137
  
 
 
