 49 
Discussion 
Interconnection governance is shifting from managing a relatively uniform grid of 
large, location-flexible fossil-fuel plants to managing a more diverse grid whose 
resources vary by technology, location, and operational characteristics. Reliability 
urgency, reflected in formal indicators, amplified by political attention, and driven by 
a changing load regime, has increased pressure to accelerate interconnection. 
Rationing policies such as RRI and ERAS respond to that pressure, but they introduce 
contested and underexamined uncertainties. How these uncertainties are managed 
matters for the clean energy industry and the broader energy transition, especially as 
expedited queues may favor a large gas build-out and reinforce utility concentration. 
Without addressing these uncertainties, the necessity and efficacy of such policies 
remain doubtful, and their longer-term consequences for interconnection governance 
remain unclear. 
The analysis of the docket, interviews, and queue shows that ongoing contested 
uncertainties and the exclusion of other uncertainties from formal discussion are 
influenced by a short-term reliability view. This view has not yet adjusted to new 
realities such as the rapid increase in hyperscaler load and the need to incorporate 
clean resources for a decarbonized future. Additionally, it has not fully considered that 
ensuring reliability might require more collective governance approaches. Based on 
this insight, I propose three ideas for adaptive learning. 
Rethink the scope of reliability. As an obligation of technocratic institutions, reliability 
is not an absolute standard but a strategic planning tool that relies on forecasts of 
future demand and supply to prepare the grid for the days ahead. Hyperscalers reach 
out to utilities to show interest in interconnection; these expressions, even with policy 
guardrails against speculation, shape utility demand forecasts, which are then 
aggregated at regional and national levels to produce reliability metrics. The 
"reliability need" that drives queue rationing, therefore, rests largely on forward-
looking projections of data center interest. Data centers provide critical computing 
infrastructure essential for technological innovation, but their unprecedented scale 
and impact on the electricity industry should not be absorbed into a long-established 
reliability concept without close examination. Adapting the reliability framework to 
explicitly manage uncertainty is necessary for policymaking that fairly serves the 
general welfare. 

 50 
Embed reliability in broader values. Reliability can easily overshadow other public 
values when it is treated as an overriding emergency claim. However, interviews 
suggest that sustainability and reliability do not have to be in conflict. As one clean 
energy advocate emphasized, no resource, including gas, is perfectly reliable. Under 
climate-driven winter storms, dependence on any single resource can itself become a 
reliability risk. Reliability is therefore not secured by favoring traditional resources, 
but through system management that supports transmission, resource diversity, and 
climate-aware planning. In a climate-stressed future, managing uncertainty requires 
reframing reliability not as a value that overrides affordability and sustainability, but 
as one that must be governed alongside them. 
Build collective reliability governance. Technocratic governance relies on expertise, 
but this case shows that its structure can also narrow the range of plausible solutions 
and limit meaningful stakeholder engagement when reliability concerns become 
urgent. Transparent and accessible processes are themselves a form of power. When 
deliberation is compressed and highly technical, actors with institutional authority 
and technical credibility tend to dominate. When the process is more visible and the 
stakes are legible to a broader public, more balanced policymaking becomes more 
possible. Managing uncertainty well requires a more collaborative posture, in which 
coalitions across the policy subsystem have both voice and sustained engagement to 
support adaptive learning. States could engage more actively in interconnection 
policy, not only as recipients of RTO proposals but also as actors that carry clean 
energy goals into the process. Upstream infrastructure policy may also benefit from 
broader public visibility. Interconnection planning remains largely invisible to most 
people, yet it shapes affordability, sustainability, and the distribution of costs among 
ratepayers. Making these consequences more visible could help broaden how 
interconnection is understood: not only as a technical bottleneck, but also as a 
publicly consequential site of energy-transition governance. 
 
 

